entry.summary:
Laravel Policies are a powerful feature for managing authorization logic in a clean, reusable way. They allow developers to define access control rules for specific models, keeping authorization logic separate from controllers and views. But are they really used in real-world projects, or are they just a shiny feature that sounds good on paper? Let’s dive into their practical use, benefits, and when you might skip them. Policies in Laravel are classes that organize authorization logic for a specific model or resource. They’re typically used with Laravel’s Gate or the authorize() method to control access to actions like viewing, creating, updating, or deleting resources. For example, a PostPolicy might define who can edit or delete a blog post.
namespace App\Policies;
use App\Models\Post;…
